## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP with partitioning and a nontrivial per-substring cost, plus backtracking to recover partitions.

## Key Concepts
- DP over partitions: dp[i][t]
- Precompute cost[l][r] in O(n^2) via two-pointer mismatches
- Backpointers for reconstruction
- Complexity: O(n^2 k) time, O(n^2) space

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPalindromePartitions(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const cost = Array.from({length: n}, ()=> Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let l = 0; l < n; l++) {
    for (let r = l; r < n; r++) {
      let a = l, b = r, c = 0;
      while (a < b) { if (s[a] !== s[b]) c++; a++; b--; }
      cost[l][r] = c;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k + 1).fill(Infinity));
  const parent = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) dp[i][1] = cost[0][i];
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t - 1; i < n; i++) {
      for (let p = t - 2; p < i; p++) {
        const val = dp[p][t - 1] + cost[p + 1][i];
        if (val < dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = val; parent[i][t] = p; }
      }
    }
  }
  const splits = [];
  let i = n - 1, t = k;
  while (t >= 1) {
    splits.push(i);
    i = parent[i][t];
    t--;
  }
  splits.reverse();
  return { minCost: dp[n - 1][k], partitions: splits };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize space for large n or small k?
- How would this adapt if cost used a different per-substring metric?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP over partitioning with a non-trivial segment value (max letter value).

## Key Concepts
- DP over partitions with exact k segments
- Precomputing segment values (maxChar)
- Reconstructing the cut points

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPartitionValue(s, k){
  const n = s.length;
  const val = [...s].map(c => c.charCodeAt(0) - 96); // a=1..z=26
  const maxChar = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(n+1).fill(0));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
    let m=0;
    for(let j=i;j<n;j++){
      m = Math.max(m, val[j]);
      maxChar[i][j+1] = m;
    }
  }
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const pre = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=Math.min(i,k);t++){
      for(let p=t-1;p<=i-1;p++){
        const cand = dp[p][t-1] + maxChar[p][i];
        if(cand > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = cand; pre[i][t] = p; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return {value: dp[n][k], cuts: []};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle k=1 or k=n in constant time?
- Can you reduce space to O(nk) without losing reconstruction capabilities?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests your ability to design a dynamic programming solution for fixed-partition subproblems. It requires careful state definition, transition logic, and the ability to handle substring-to-integer conversion efficiently. Additionally, storing cut pointers enables reconstruction of the optimal partition.

## Key Concepts

- **Partition DP**: Divide a linear sequence into exactly k segments
- **On-the-fly value computation**: Accumulate digit values incrementally without re-parsing substrings
- **Reconstruction**: Maintain a parent pointer array to recover the cut positions
- **Time/Space tradeoffs**: O(n²k) time complexity with O(nk) space; can optimize to O(nk) space using rolling arrays

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxSumPartition(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const digits = s.split('').map(Number);

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const cut = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= Math.min(i, k); t++) {
      let val = 0;
      for (let j = i; j >= t; j--) {
        val = digits[j - 1] * Math.pow(10, i - j) + val;
        if (dp[j - 1][t - 1] !== -Infinity) {
          const sum = dp[j - 1][t - 1] + val;
          if (sum > dp[i][t]) {
            dp[i][t] = sum;
            cut[i][t] = j - 1;
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const cuts = [];
  let pos = n;
  for (let t = k; t >= 1; t--) {
    cuts.push(cut[pos][t]);
    pos = cut[pos][t];
  }

  return { maxSum: dp[n][k], cutPositions: cuts.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests clean 0/1 DP layering with an additional teleport operation, requiring careful handling to avoid double-counting and efficient O(mn) time complexity with minimal extra memory overhead.

## Key Concepts
- 0/1 DP layering with an additional operation constraint
- Row/column prefix maxima for efficient teleport transition computation
- Path reconstruction under dual-state DP representation

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSum(grid) {
  const m = grid.length, n = grid[0].length;
  const dp0 = Array(m).fill().map(() => Array(n).fill(0));
  const dp1 = Array(m).fill().map(() => Array(n).fill(0));

  // Initialize first cell
  dp0[0][0] = grid[0][0];
  dp1[0][0] = grid[0][0];

  // Fill DP tables
  for (let i = 0; i < m; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < n; j++) {
      if (i === 0 && j === 0) continue;

      // Regular moves without teleport
      dp0[i][j] = Math.max(
        i > 0 ? dp0[i-1][j] : 0,
        j > 0 ? dp0[i][j-1] : 0
      ) + grid[i][j];

      // After teleport or using teleport now
      dp1[i][j] = Math.max(
        i > 0 ? dp1[i-1][j] : 0,
        j > 0 ? dp1[i][j-1] : 0,
        // Teleport from any cell in same row/column
        ...getTeleportOptions(dp0, i, j)
      ) + grid[i][j];
    }
  }

  return Math.max(dp0[m-1][n-1], dp1[m-1][n-1]);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to model interval scheduling with a cap on concurrent starts, a common real-world constraint in job scheduling and ad campaigns.

## Key Concepts
- Weighted interval scheduling with a limit on number of intervals
- Preprocessing next[i] via binary search
- DP with reconstruction pointers for actual selection

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode sketch for dp and reconstruction
function maxProfitTasks(s, d, p, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const idx = [...Array(n).keys()].sort((a,b)=> s[a]-s[b]);
  // reorder s,d,p by idx as needed; compute next[] with binary search
  const next = Array(n).fill(n);
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++) {
    let limit = s[i] + d[i] - 1;
    // binary search for first j with s[j] > limit
    let lo=i+1, hi=n; while (lo<hi){
      let mid=(lo+hi)>>1; if (s[mid] > limit) hi=mid; else lo=mid+1; }
    next[i] = lo;
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(0));
  const take = Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(false));
  for (let i=n-1;i>=0;i--) {
    for (let t=1;t<=k;t++) {
      if (dp[i+1][t] >= p[i] + dp[next[i]][t-1]) {
        dp[i][t] = dp[i+1][t];
        take[i][t] = false;
      } else {
        dp[i][t] = p[i] + dp[next[i]][t-1];
        take[i][t] = true;
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return {profit: dp[0][k], take, next};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests DP with a non-adjacent removal constraint and requires reconstruction of which indices were removed.

## Key Concepts

- 3D DP state dp[i][t][r]
- Non-adjacent constraint during removal
- Path reconstruction for the chosen indices

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxRemainingSum(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = Number.NEGATIVE_INFINITY / 4;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () =>
    Array.from({ length: k + 1 }, () => [NEG, NEG])
  );
  dp[0][0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      // keep i
      dp[i][t][0] = Math.max(dp[i-1][t][0], dp[i-1][t][1]) + A[i-1];
      // remove i
      if (t > 0) dp[i][t][1] = dp[i-1][t-1][0];
    }
  }
  return Math.max(dp[n][k][0], dp[n][k][1]);
}
```

